From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 21:14:19 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] i2c: qcom-cci: Retry a message that timed out

On the Galaxy Tab S3 the pull-ups of both CCI buses are powered with
the camera sensors: with both sensors off, SDA and SCL read low. When
a sensor is powered off and on again while the CCI stays runtime
active, the first transfer after the power-up hangs in about a third
of the cycles: the queue stops at the write command, no interrupt
arrives, and after 100 ms the driver reports

  i2c-qcom-cci a0c000.cci: master 0 queue 0 timeout

With the rail held up by the other sensor, or with the CCI suspended
while the bus is unpowered, the failures go away. imx258 sends its
software reset as the first write after power-up, so stream starts
fail with "imx258_start_streaming failed to reset sensor".

The timeout path already resets and re-initialises the controller, and
the transfer after that succeeds. Retry a message once when it timed
out, and log the timeout only when the retry times out as well.
Samsung's GPL kernel avoids the state by resetting the master each
time a sensor is powered up (msm_cci_init() in
drivers/media/platform/msm/camera_v2/sensor/cci/msm_cci.c), which the
CCI driver cannot do as it does not know when its clients are powered.

Tested on a Galaxy Tab S3 (APQ8096): 120 imx258 power cycles with a
write straight after power-up, 43 first attempts timed out, none of
the retries did; 40 camera stream starts without an error.

Signed-off-by: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c | 36 +++++++++++++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c
index bd70c6bae5..9ab8b5c247 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-qcom-cci.c
@@ -301,7 +301,7 @@ static int cci_run_queue(struct cci *cci, u8 master, u8 queue)
 
 	if (!wait_for_completion_timeout(&cci->master[master].irq_complete,
 					 CCI_TIMEOUT)) {
-		dev_err(cci->dev, "master %d queue %d timeout\n",
+		dev_dbg(cci->dev, "master %d queue %d timeout\n",
 			master, queue);
 		cci_reset(cci);
 		return -ETIMEDOUT;
@@ -418,6 +418,14 @@ static int cci_i2c_write(struct cci *cci, u16 master,
 	return cci_run_queue(cci, master, queue);
 }
 
+static int cci_xfer_msg(struct cci *cci, u16 master, struct i2c_msg *msg)
+{
+	if (msg->flags & I2C_M_RD)
+		return cci_i2c_read(cci, master, msg->addr, msg->buf, msg->len);
+
+	return cci_i2c_write(cci, master, msg->addr, msg->buf, msg->len);
+}
+
 static int cci_xfer(struct i2c_adapter *adap, struct i2c_msg msgs[], int num)
 {
 	struct cci_master *cci_master = i2c_get_adapdata(adap);
@@ -429,17 +437,23 @@ static int cci_xfer(struct i2c_adapter *adap, struct i2c_msg msgs[], int num)
 		goto err;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < num; i++) {
-		if (msgs[i].flags & I2C_M_RD)
-			ret = cci_i2c_read(cci, cci_master->master,
-					   msgs[i].addr, msgs[i].buf,
-					   msgs[i].len);
-		else
-			ret = cci_i2c_write(cci, cci_master->master,
-					    msgs[i].addr, msgs[i].buf,
-					    msgs[i].len);
-
-		if (ret < 0)
+		ret = cci_xfer_msg(cci, cci_master->master, &msgs[i]);
+
+		/*
+		 * A master can hang on its next transfer when the bus lost
+		 * power while it was idle, e.g. with pull-ups powered together
+		 * with the client device. The timeout has reset the
+		 * controller, so try once more.
+		 */
+		if (ret == -ETIMEDOUT)
+			ret = cci_xfer_msg(cci, cci_master->master, &msgs[i]);
+
+		if (ret < 0) {
+			if (ret == -ETIMEDOUT)
+				dev_err(cci->dev, "master %d timeout\n",
+					cci_master->master);
 			break;
+		}
 	}
 
 	if (!ret)
